### Issue description

### Description

Opening the Jade configuration screen (WailaConfigScreen) causes a client crash when the narrator system attempts to generate narration text for a keybind option.

Jade passes a StringWidget instance as an argument to Component.translatable(), which is not a valid argument type.

Minecraft only accepts the following argument types for translatable components:
Component
String
Number
Boolean

Passing any other type results in an IllegalArgumentException.

## Crash Log (excerpt)
```java
java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: TranslatableContents' arguments must be either a Component, Number, Boolean, or a String. Was given net.minecraft.client.gui.components.StringWidget@72ba0e3b for narrator.controls.unbound
    at net.minecraft.network.chat.contents.TranslatableContents.<init>(TranslatableContents.java:85)
    at net.minecraft.network.chat.Component.translatable(Component.java:144)
    at snownee.jade.gui.config.KeybindOptionButton.lambda$new$1(KeybindOptionButton.java:25)
```
## Steps to Reproduce
Launch Minecraft with Jade installed
Open the Jade configuration menu
Navigate to the keybind options
Observe immediate crash when narration updates

## Expected Behavior
Narration should generate valid text without crashing, even when narration is enabled.

## Actual Behavior
Client crashes due to an invalid argument type being passed into Component.translatable() during narration generation.

## Root Cause
In KeybindOptionButton, the narration lambda passes a StringWidget instance directly into:
Component.translatable("narrator.controls.unbound", widget)

This violates Minecraft’s requirement that translatable arguments must be:
Component
String
Number
Boolean

Any other type triggers a runtime exception during component construction.

## Proposed Fix
Ensure all arguments passed into Component.translatable() are sanitized before use:

```java
Object arg = widget;

if (arg instanceof StringWidget sw) {
    arg = sw.getMessage().getString();
}

Component.translatable("narrator.controls.unbound", arg);
```
Alternatively, defensively sanitize all arguments:


```java
for (int i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    Object o = args[i];

    if (!(o instanceof String ||
          o instanceof Number ||
          o instanceof Boolean ||
          o instanceof Component)) {
        args[i] = String.valueOf(o);
    }
}
```

## Additional Notes
The crash can occur even when narration is not explicitly used, as narration callbacks are still executed internally.
The issue is reproducible on multiple NeoForge 26.1.x environments.
A mixin-based workaround that sanitizes arguments confirms the issue and prevents the crash.

This is the mixin
```java
package net.capy.machinemod.mixin;

import net.minecraft.network.chat.Component;
import net.minecraft.client.gui.components.StringWidget;
import org.spongepowered.asm.mixin.Mixin;
import org.spongepowered.asm.mixin.injection.At;
import org.spongepowered.asm.mixin.injection.ModifyArg;
import snownee.jade.gui.config.KeybindOptionButton;

@Mixin(KeybindOptionButton.class)
public class JadeKeybindFixMixin {

    @ModifyArg(
            method = "lambda$new$1",
            at = @At(
                    value = "INVOKE",
                    target = "Lnet/minecraft/network/chat/Component;translatable(Ljava/lang/String;[Ljava/lang/Object;)Lnet/minecraft/network/chat/MutableComponent;"
            ),
            index = 1
    )
    private Object[] jade_fixInvalidNarrationArgs(Object[] originalArgs) {
        Object[] fixed = new Object[originalArgs.length];

        for (int i = 0; i < originalArgs.length; i++) {
            Object arg = originalArgs[i];

            if (arg instanceof StringWidget widget) {
                // Convert the widget to a readable string
                fixed[i] = widget.getMessage().getString();
            } else if (
                    !(arg instanceof Component) &&
                            !(arg instanceof String) &&
                            !(arg instanceof Number) &&
                            !(arg instanceof Boolean)
            ) {
                // Fallback for any other invalid type
                fixed[i] = String.valueOf(arg);
            } else {
                fixed[i] = arg;
            }
        }

        return fixed;
    }
}
```

