### Issue description

**Problem:**

Starting from Minecraft version 1.21.5, the method `net.minecraft.nbt.CompoundTag#getInt(String)` has been modified to return an `Optional<Integer>` instead of a primitive `int`. This change causes the signal level displayed by the comparator and calibrated sculk sensor to show as `Optional[signal]`, which is identical to the `toString()` representation of `java.util.Optional`.

```java
/** Since 1.21.5 */
public Optional<Integer> getInt(String string) {
    return this.getOptional(string).flatMap(Tag::asInt);
}
```

**Impact:**

This results in the following issues:
1. **Comparator Block:** The signal strength in the tooltip for the comparator is incorrectly displayed as `Optional[signal]`.
2. **Calibrated Sculk Sensor:** Similarly, the tooltip for the calibrated sculk sensor also shows the signal as `Optional[signal]`, leading to the same confusion.

**Code Section Affected:**

```java
@Override
public void appendTooltip(ITooltip tooltip, BlockAccessor accessor, IPluginConfig config) {
	BlockState state = accessor.getBlockState();
	Block block = state.getBlock();
	IThemeHelper t = IThemeHelper.get();

	// Unrelated code...

	if (block == Blocks.COMPARATOR) {
		ComparatorMode mode = state.getValue(BlockStateProperties.MODE_COMPARATOR);
		Component modeInfo = t.info(Component.translatable(
				"tooltip.jade.mode_" + (mode == ComparatorMode.COMPARE ? "comparator" : "subtractor")));
		tooltip.add(Component.translatable("tooltip.jade.mode", modeInfo));
		if (accessor.getServerData().contains("Signal")) {
			tooltip.add(Component.translatable("tooltip.jade.power", t.info(accessor.getServerData().getInt("Signal")))); // returns Optional<Integer>
		}
		return;
	}

	if (block instanceof CalibratedSculkSensorBlock && accessor.getServerData().contains("Signal")) {
		tooltip.add(Component.translatable("jade.input_signal", t.info(accessor.getServerData().getInt("Signal")))); // returns Optional<Integer>
	}

	// Unrelated code...
}
```

In the code above, the call to `accessor.getServerData().getInt("Signal")` now returns an `Optional<Integer>`, which is not handled properly when added to the tooltip. This results in the display showing an `Optional[signal]` instead of just the signal value.

**Suggested Fix:**

To resolve this issue, the code needs to unwrap the `Optional<Integer>` and display the actual signal value if present. A possible fix could involve modifying the tooltip addition logic to check if the `Optional` contains a value, and only then display the signal strength:

```java
if (accessor.getServerData().contains("Signal")) {
    Optional<Integer> signalOpt = accessor.getServerData().getInt("Signal");
    signalOpt.ifPresent(signal -> tooltip.add(Component.translatable("tooltip.jade.power", t.info(signal))));
}
```

**Additional Information:**

This bug has been tested on Fabric, and it is currently unknown whether it also affects the NeoForge version. Attached are screenshots showing the incorrect display of the signal as `Optional[signal]`.

